[[:db/retract 17592186198689 :question/answer-fields 17592186194760] {:db/id 17592186198099 :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\land.$\" \n\nSo, the first sentence reads\n\n![](images/0c/0c81e18633c3089e7c9f56464738a57dae490c70a0c8b46d116b1a8d75cd433f.svg)\n\nthat is, $A \\land B.$\n\nMoreover, the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor.$\" \n\nSo, the second sentence reads\n\n![](images/0a/0a76866dfc078172503784c3d67c7d36abbfe3362a9af3e07e6055f168debbf4.svg)\n\nthat is, $A \\lor C.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198253 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-17d0f2d8-abc7-53ff-a964-9b193b159c69"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198254 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-82d493f7-be8a-5b02-922e-c52bcfe9d8a4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198255 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-be776820-4de7-562d-ad85-1f2cc185142d" "question-0d9b6544-a3d6-5482-a66b-0b543948f593"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-17d0f2d8-abc7-53ff-a964-9b193b159c69" :question/id #uuid "17d0f2d8-abc7-53ff-a964-9b193b159c69" :question/math-academy-id "q-170742" :question/problem "$n$ is less than $10$ **or** $13$, **and** $n$ is an integer\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $X(n):$ $n$ is less than $10$\n\n- $Y(n):$ $n$ is less than $13$\n\n- $Z(n):$ $n$ is an integer" :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:  \n\n- **Part 1:** $n$ is less than $10$ or $13.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{n\\text{ is less than }10}_{⏟}}_{X}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{n\\text{ is less than }13}_{⏟}}_{Y},\n$$\n\nthat is, $X∨Y.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $n$ is an integer. This corresponds to $Z.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{n\\text{ is less than }10\\text{ or }13}_{⏟}}_{X∨Y}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{n\\text{ is an integer}}_{⏟}}_{Z},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(X∨Y)∧Z.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cb8b0f6c-4a71-5c9b-bc62-54e3ad24cf0e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cb8b0f6c-4a71-5c9b-bc62-54e3ad24cf0e" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb8b0f6c-4a71-5c9b-bc62-54e3ad24cf0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-975840e4-95b6-570a-bca6-50be4fce0c45" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c69e7e45-595b-57f9-9f1e-7da09afbdb28" "answer-975840e4-95b6-570a-bca6-50be4fce0c45" "answer-19fe9774-58ed-565f-9cbb-223d3c49f77e" "answer-f8d36d58-81e3-5ce6-a949-0b1fb3544fff" "answer-bda4b648-d128-5a9f-af66-b574da891c6b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c69e7e45-595b-57f9-9f1e-7da09afbdb28" :answer/id #uuid "c69e7e45-595b-57f9-9f1e-7da09afbdb28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(X∧Y)∨Z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-975840e4-95b6-570a-bca6-50be4fce0c45" :answer/id #uuid "975840e4-95b6-570a-bca6-50be4fce0c45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(X∨Y)∧Z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19fe9774-58ed-565f-9cbb-223d3c49f77e" :answer/id #uuid "19fe9774-58ed-565f-9cbb-223d3c49f77e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(X∨Y)∨Z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8d36d58-81e3-5ce6-a949-0b1fb3544fff" :answer/id #uuid "f8d36d58-81e3-5ce6-a949-0b1fb3544fff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "X∧(Y∧Z)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bda4b648-d128-5a9f-af66-b574da891c6b" :answer/id #uuid "bda4b648-d128-5a9f-af66-b574da891c6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "X∨(Y∧Z)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-82d493f7-be8a-5b02-922e-c52bcfe9d8a4" :question/id #uuid "82d493f7-be8a-5b02-922e-c52bcfe9d8a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-175552" :question/problem "Find the truth set of $P(x)∧Q(x)$ given the following predicates defined over the set of positive integers:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is odd\n\n- $Q(x):$ $12$ is divisible by $x$" :question/worked-solution "The truth set of $P(x)∧Q(x)$ represents the *intersection* of the truth sets of $P(x)$ and $Q(x):$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P∧Q}={T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}\n$$\n\nWe start by finding the truth sets of $P(x)$ and $Q(x):$ \n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is the set of all positive odd integers, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\{1,3,5,7,9,11,13…\\}.\n$$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n\n$$\n{T}_{Q}=\\{1,2,3,4,6,12\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P∧Q} & ={T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} \\\\  & =\\{1,3,5,7,9,11,13…\\}\\cap \\{1,2,3,4,6,12\\} \\\\  & =\\{1,3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6b76c296-5c89-50a5-b725-0525be121d8e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6b76c296-5c89-50a5-b725-0525be121d8e" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b76c296-5c89-50a5-b725-0525be121d8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a9428b0b-b88e-5b0f-a9db-05a9a71468a8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b35083dd-72dd-533d-9726-6630eec2aaae" "answer-46ea5f59-2e7f-5295-8695-9592bd42ab82" "answer-a9428b0b-b88e-5b0f-a9db-05a9a71468a8" "answer-550263d1-e585-5aa0-8873-ca1a37d6d639" "answer-9b251db1-3373-5f6f-983a-96f7fe8666bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b35083dd-72dd-533d-9726-6630eec2aaae" :answer/id #uuid "b35083dd-72dd-533d-9726-6630eec2aaae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{1,2,3,4,6,12\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46ea5f59-2e7f-5295-8695-9592bd42ab82" :answer/id #uuid "46ea5f59-2e7f-5295-8695-9592bd42ab82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{1,3,5,7,9\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9428b0b-b88e-5b0f-a9db-05a9a71468a8" :answer/id #uuid "a9428b0b-b88e-5b0f-a9db-05a9a71468a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{1,3\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-550263d1-e585-5aa0-8873-ca1a37d6d639" :answer/id #uuid "550263d1-e585-5aa0-8873-ca1a37d6d639" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{3,6\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b251db1-3373-5f6f-983a-96f7fe8666bd" :answer/id #uuid "9b251db1-3373-5f6f-983a-96f7fe8666bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All positive odd integers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-be776820-4de7-562d-ad85-1f2cc185142d" :question/id #uuid "be776820-4de7-562d-ad85-1f2cc185142d" :question/math-academy-id "q-177759" :question/problem "Simplify the following sentence: \"$z$ is rational and it is a rational number, or $z$ is rational or it is a positive number.\"" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the phrase \"$z$ is rational and it is a rational number\" to just \"$z$ is rational.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$z$ is rational, or $z$ is rational or it is a positive number.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma in the sentence:\n\n\"$z$ is rational or $z$ is rational, or it is a positive number.\"\n\nNow, we simplify the phrase \"$z$ is rational or $z$ is rational\" to just \"$z$ is rational.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$z$ is rational, or it is a positive number.\"\n\nFinally, we simplify the sentence to\n\n\"$z$ is rational or positive.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cd4aac9d-4227-5875-926d-41c64f7175cf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cd4aac9d-4227-5875-926d-41c64f7175cf" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd4aac9d-4227-5875-926d-41c64f7175cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9046c2c1-c0ee-5e13-92fe-5436918ed8ee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c455468d-3494-592f-8fea-f7eb970fc2bd" "answer-91799f63-a171-5d71-abd6-d2ac6eb622bc" "answer-a892fa0b-ae64-5844-bb4e-4a9d0eb3ece7" "answer-9046c2c1-c0ee-5e13-92fe-5436918ed8ee" "answer-46192eea-f4ae-5745-ab2b-1935f29c22a2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c455468d-3494-592f-8fea-f7eb970fc2bd" :answer/id #uuid "c455468d-3494-592f-8fea-f7eb970fc2bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$z$ is positive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91799f63-a171-5d71-abd6-d2ac6eb622bc" :answer/id #uuid "91799f63-a171-5d71-abd6-d2ac6eb622bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$z$ is rational" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a892fa0b-ae64-5844-bb4e-4a9d0eb3ece7" :answer/id #uuid "a892fa0b-ae64-5844-bb4e-4a9d0eb3ece7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$z$ is rational and positive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9046c2c1-c0ee-5e13-92fe-5436918ed8ee" :answer/id #uuid "9046c2c1-c0ee-5e13-92fe-5436918ed8ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$z$ is rational or positive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46192eea-f4ae-5745-ab2b-1935f29c22a2" :answer/id #uuid "46192eea-f4ae-5745-ab2b-1935f29c22a2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$z$ is rational or positive but not both" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0d9b6544-a3d6-5482-a66b-0b543948f593" :question/id #uuid "0d9b6544-a3d6-5482-a66b-0b543948f593" :question/math-academy-id "q-171040" :question/problem "Simplify the following sentence: \"$y$ is irrational and it is an irrational number, or $y$ is irrational or it is a negative number.\"" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the phrase \"$y$ is irrational and it is an irrational number\" to just \"$y$ is irrational.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$y$ is irrational, or $y$ is irrational or it is a negative number.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma in the sentence:\n\n\"$y$ is irrational or $y$ is irrational, or it is a negative number.\"\n\nNow, we simplify the phrase \"$y$ is irrational or $y$ is irrational\" to just \"$y$ is irrational.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$y$ is irrational, or it is a negative number.\"\n\nFinally, we simplify the sentence to\n\n\"$y$ is irrational or negative.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ef163c6e-9635-5101-b558-ea7a832bcdd4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ef163c6e-9635-5101-b558-ea7a832bcdd4" :answer-field/id #uuid "ef163c6e-9635-5101-b558-ea7a832bcdd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7d17b8f6-c501-5b6a-9f25-24fb0da0b825" :answer-field/choices ["answer-97073a41-e9c9-5ba0-a4bd-c3da3d9761ba" "answer-5350a4a4-f298-59d8-9612-4c7517461dce" "answer-7d17b8f6-c501-5b6a-9f25-24fb0da0b825" "answer-8724cd76-5bf1-5bcc-bde0-4e58af383bf0" "answer-ae7c50ca-e9d1-5f38-8f63-d041811dcbb9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97073a41-e9c9-5ba0-a4bd-c3da3d9761ba" :answer/id #uuid "97073a41-e9c9-5ba0-a4bd-c3da3d9761ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y$ is irrational and negative." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5350a4a4-f298-59d8-9612-4c7517461dce" :answer/id #uuid "5350a4a4-f298-59d8-9612-4c7517461dce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y$ is irrational or negative but not both." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d17b8f6-c501-5b6a-9f25-24fb0da0b825" :answer/id #uuid "7d17b8f6-c501-5b6a-9f25-24fb0da0b825" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y$ is irrational or negative." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8724cd76-5bf1-5bcc-bde0-4e58af383bf0" :answer/id #uuid "8724cd76-5bf1-5bcc-bde0-4e58af383bf0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y$ is irrational." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae7c50ca-e9d1-5f38-8f63-d041811dcbb9" :answer/id #uuid "ae7c50ca-e9d1-5f38-8f63-d041811dcbb9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y$ is negative." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199520 :tutorial/content "We can apply the logical operations of conjunction and disjunction to predicates.\n\n- The **conjunction** of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is a new predicate which we denote as follows: \n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\land Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)\n$$\n\nThis conjunction is true whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true *and* $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is true for a specific set of respective variable assignments. The conjunction is false if either $P$ or $Q$ is false.\n\n- The **disjunction** of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is written as follows:\n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\lor Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)\n$$\n\nThis disjunction is true whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true, $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is true, or both are true, for a specific set of variable assignments. The disjunction is false if $P$ *and* $Q$ are false.\n\nFor example, consider the following predicates:\n\n- $P(n):$ $n$ is even\n\n- $Q(n):$ $n$ is positive\n\nThen, $P(n) \\land Q(n)$ is a new predicate that reads \"$n$ is even *and* $n$ is positive,\" which can be simplified to \"$n$ is even and positive.\"\n\nOn the other hand, $P(n) \\lor Q(n)$ is a new predicate that reads \"$n$ is even *or* $n$ is positive,\" which can be simplified to \"$n$ is even or positive.\"\n\nWhen it's clear that $P(n)$ is a predicate in the variable $n,$ we sometimes omit the variables when writing them. So, $P(n) \\land Q(n)$ and $P(n) \\lor Q(n)$ can be written as $P \\land Q$ and $P \\lor Q,$ respectively." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199521 :tutorial/content "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Note that we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nFrom the definition of the conjunction of predicates, we have that $P \\land Q$ is true if both $P$ and $Q$ are true. Therefore, the truth set of $P \\land Q$ is the *intersection* of the truth sets of $P$ and $Q{:}$\n\n$$\nT_{P \\land Q} = T_P \\cap T_Q\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following predicates defined over the set of positive integers:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is divisible by $3$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $24$ is divisible by $x$\n\nWe start by finding the truth sets of $P(x)$ and $Q(x){:}$\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is \n\n$$\nT_P = \\big\\{ 3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,27 \\ldots \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n\n$$\nT_Q = \\big\\{ 1,2,3,4,6,8,12,24 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P∧Q} & ={T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} \\\\  & =\\{3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,27…\\}\\cap \\{1,2,3,4,6,8,12,24\\} \\\\  & =\\{3,6,12,24\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199542 :tutorial/content "Once again, let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. \n\nFrom the definition of the disjunction of predicates, we have that $P \\lor Q$ is true if $P$ is true, or $Q$ is true, or both are true. Therefore, the truth set of $P \\lor Q$ must be the *union* of the truth sets of $P$ and $Q{:}$\n\n$$\nT_{P \\lor Q} = T_P \\cup T_Q\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the following shaded regions that represent the truth sets of the predicates $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y),$ defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ \n\n![](images/ce/ce71e62503acd1c82f6bba7e1d9c83c40f0a98f04c689c3ccd58e7a99a814856.png)\n\nTo get the truth set of $P(x,y) \\lor Q(x,y),$ we take all the points that belong to $T_P$ or $T_Q,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/9c/9c528c5fe390bd1f22cb473c01b768e83f5b3a35d97eb7e670c6734b8cb2c2f8.png)\n\n**Note:** The points where the *dashed* line meets the *solid* curve belong to $T_P \\cup T_Q$ since these points lie in $T_P.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199523 :tutorial/content "Conjunction and disjunction of predicates are generalizations of logical operations on statements. Moreover, the properties of these operations on statements can be translated to corresponding operations on predicates. \n\nIn particular, the idempotent, absorption, associative, and commutative laws also apply to conjunction and disjunction of predicates. \n\nSo, for predicates $P(x_1,x_2\\ldots,x_n),$ $Q(y_1,y_2\\ldots,y_m),$ and $R(z_1,z_2\\ldots,z_p),$ we have the following:\n\n- *The Idempotent Laws*: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧P≡P∨P≡P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- *The Associative Laws*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∧R) & ≡(P∧Q)∧R \\\\ P∨(Q∨R) & ≡(P∨Q)∨R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- *The Commutative Laws*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧Q & ≡Q∧P \\\\ P∨Q & ≡Q∨P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- *The Distributive Laws*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∨R) & ≡(P∧Q)∨(P∧R) \\\\ P∨(Q∧R) & ≡(P∨Q)∧(P∨R)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- *The Absorption Laws*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(P∨Q) & ≡P \\\\ P∨(P∧Q) & ≡P\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198514 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
